"""Single source of truth for SDK version. Bump on every src change.
Per repo CLAUDE.md: also update LATEST_SDK_VERSIONS['onelo-python'] in
frontend/lib/sdk-version.ts (Task 12).

Note: Python uses PEP 440 versioning (not semver suffixes). The `0.1.0a1`
form is the PEP 440 equivalent of "alpha 1 / staging" — sorts before
`0.1.0` final and is accepted by hatchling/pip. The `-staging` suffix used
in JS/Swift/Android SDKs is invalid here.
"""
# IMPORTANT: keep the inline comment free of double-quotes — the Publish SDK
# workflow extracts the FIRST quoted string on this line as the version.
__version__ = "0.5.0a39"  # was 0.5.0a38; auth polish. (A6) verify_token/_sync now reject a 200 response with no usable user id (malformed backend response no longer passes as an authenticated empty user). (A4) accept_query_token parity — the ASGI/WSGI middlewares, Flask decorators, Django DRF auth class, and Litestar guard now support authenticating via ?token= (opt-in, default off) for SSE/EventSource clients that can't set an Authorization header; previously only FastAPI. (A5 negative-caching deferred — needs a cache-Protocol extension for a low-severity gain; better designed into @onelo/node.) (a38 was: auth A1/A2/A3.) Prior context — auth backend hardening. (A1) the universal ASGI/WSGI middleware now distinguishes "backend unreachable" from "no/invalid token": on OneloAuthUnavailable it sets scope['onelo_auth_unavailable']/environ['onelo.auth_unavailable']=True (user still None) so an Onelo outage can't silently degrade to anonymous access. (A2) verify_token/_sync now reuse a pooled keep-alive httpx client (no TLS handshake per verification) — ephemeral only when a test transport is injected. (A3) a 429 maps to new OneloAuthRateLimited (subclass of Unavailable, carries retry_after) and the retry loops no longer retry-storm a rate-limit. Status mapping unified in _map_response so async/sync can't drift. (a37 was: monitor event_ts on the wire.) Prior context — monitor events now send the real event time on the wire (`ts`, ISO-8601 UTC captured at MonitorEvent creation) so a batched/retried/atexit-drained event isn't mis-dated to ingest time (M7). Backend stores it as a display-only, clamped `event_ts` (dashboard shows event_ts ?? created_at); created_at stays server-now() and remains authoritative for all windowing/quota/incident/retention/cursor. (a36 was: monitor sampling + quota self-throttle + drop signal.) Prior context — (M4) client-side sampling via monitor.init(sample_rate=, success_sample_rate=) — errors vs successes sampled independently, default 1.0 (no change), decided before scope/scrub/transport so an error storm can't thrash the buffer. (M5) transport now reads X-Onelo-Quota-Remaining and self-throttles: preemptive hold-off when remaining hits 0, info log when low, quota_remaining exposed. (M6) dropped-event count is now surfaced via a rate-limited warning (once per newly-dropped batch) instead of climbing silently. (a35 was: monitor fork-safety + release/env placement + WSGI streaming.) Prior context — (M1) transport thread is now rebuilt after fork via os.register_at_fork -> MonitorTransport.reinit_after_fork, so gunicorn/uwsgi preload workers actually deliver events instead of buffering-then-losing them. (M2) release/environment are now stamped onto meta.app.version + meta.environment (the paths the backend aggregates on) not only meta.tags.*, so the dashboard Release/Environment filters populate for Python events. (M3) the WSGI middleware streams the response body (generator) instead of list()-materialising it, fixing broken SSE/streaming/large downloads. (a34 was: _UserSnapshotCache bounded.) Prior context — _UserSnapshotCache (for_user) is now bounded — expired entries are deleted on the get() that finds them (were leaking until the same user returned), and put() enforces a hard cap (_USER_SNAPSHOT_MAX_ENTRIES=10000) by purging expired then evicting soonest-to-expire. Fixes an unbounded memory growth on backends resolving a long tail of one-shot user_ids. (a33 was: Feature surfaces upsell metadata; cache stores full wire state.) Prior context — Feature now surfaces the backend's upsell metadata (reason, required_plan, required_plan_label, upgrade_cta + computed upgrade_hint) so server-rendered apps can build an Available-in-Pro CTA. The feature cache stores the FULL wire state dict per feature (was status-only); SSE/poll/for_user keep it end-to-end; snapshot() still returns status-only for the monitor flag provider. Back-compat: replace_all normalises a bare status string to {status: str}. (a32 was: is_visible fail-closed for unknown status + is_known; _after_fork propagates feature_environment; polling handles up_to_date + discovery_requested.)
